 "states": [
  {
   "date": "2026-09-14",
   "text": [
    "As of 14 September the money is still arriving and the first wobble has appeared. Oracle's Q1 FY27 reported cloud infrastructure revenue up 121% to $7.4bn, remaining performance obligations of $664bn and capex of $28.5bn. The Pentagon is in talks to lend AI cloud firm Fluidstack $5bn; SoftBank sealed an upsized $11.87bn two-year loan from about 20 banks for its OpenAI investment; Cohere is in advanced talks to raise US$2bn–US$3bn at a US$20bn valuation; Discovery Loop is seeking funding at about $50bn; Z.ai filed in Hong Kong to raise about $5bn; and Nscale is seeking up to $3.5bn before a fall IPO. The DOJ is investigating whether Nvidia structured its ~$20bn Groq licensing deal to avoid antitrust review.",
    "The listings diverge. Reuters reports Nvidia is in talks to invest up to $10bn as anchor investor in an Anthropic IPO seeking up to $100bn; CNBC reports Anthropic picked the Nasdaq for a listing that CNBC says may seek a $2 trillion valuation, and the FT reports Anthropic told shareholders adjusted operating income will be positive for a second straight quarter. Altman ruled out an OpenAI listing in 2026, citing safety concerns. On 14 September AI and chip stocks sold off in Asia and US premarket after lab CEOs called for slowing development, and Samsung and SK hynix rejected KEPCO's proposal to prepay five years of electricity bills for chip clusters."
   ],
   "changed": null
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-09-21",
   "text": [
    "As of 21 September the capital is still arriving and the first full forecast of the burn is public. Crusoe raised $3.9 billion at a $30.9 billion valuation, ten months after raising $1.38 billion at a $10 billion valuation; CoreWeave announced $3.0 billion of convertible senior notes due 2033 plus an at-the-market programme of up to 35 million shares; ten banks provided US$22 billion of debt to Crux AI, the Blackstone-Alphabet venture, secured on Google TPUs and Crux AI's anticipated customer contracts; and Generac's Form 8-K shows a warrant for up to 1,693,745 shares issued to an Amazon subsidiary against payments of up to $8 billion for data-centre backup generators.",
    "Two sets of figures came from documents seen by one newspaper each. Reuters, reporting the Financial Times' account of an OpenAI presentation, says the company expects to burn $278 billion in cash between 2026 and 2030, spending about $856 billion on computing power and infrastructure by the end of 2030 while revenue rises from $36 billion this year to $350 billion in 2030; and the Financial Times reported technology companies have provided as much as $300 billion in guarantees financing AI data centres and chips over the past year. Nscale filed to list on the NYSE with a $1.02 billion net loss on $140.6 million of revenue in the six months to 30 June 2026, $56.4 billion in remaining performance obligations and more than $8 billion in debt. Anthropic pushed its listing to November, with backers projecting $110 billion in annualized revenue by year-end, and CNBC reported that Anthropic and OpenAI are now also pursuing 20-30 MW capacity deals in the UK, the Nordics and the US. No audited figure has been published for any of the revenue or burn projections."
   ],
   "changed": "The listing question moved from Nasdaq choice to a dated delay — Anthropic pushed to November for third-quarter results — and the first full spending forecast became public, with the Financial Times reporting OpenAI expects $278 billion of negative cash flow to 2030 and as much as $300 billion of industry guarantees keeping much of the build-out off balance sheets."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-09-28",
   "text": [
    "As of 28 September the capital is still arriving and the price of it has risen. SoftBank raised roughly $11 billion across dollars and euros, selling $1 billion of 3.5-year bonds at 8.625%, $4.5 billion of 5.5-year securities at 9.25% and a $4.5 billion 7.5-year tranche at 9.75%; The Japan Times, citing Bloomberg, says the yields are the highest-ever for SoftBank dollar bonds, on a day when benchmark US Treasury rates soared to two-decade highs, and that part of the money funds a follow-on investment in OpenAI. CNBC reported Treasury yields at their highest levels since 2007, with the 10-year near 5.17%, and cited JPMorgan's June estimate of $4.1 trillion in AI-related debt through 2030. Akamai announced an $11.6 billion seven-year commitment from Anthropic with a warrant for up to about 5% of its stock, Nscale secured $3.36 billion in convertible notes led by Third Point with $1 billion from Nvidia, and Oracle sent a force majeure notice to Blue Owl over the 2.5GW Project Jupiter campus while saying publicly that the project remains on its planned schedule.",
    "The listing question moved on structure rather than date. Reuters, citing The Information, reported Anthropic is seeking shareholder approval for a special share class giving its seven co-founders 50.1% of voting power ahead of an IPO, holding as long as three of the seven retain a minimum number of shares. Nscale's filing shows Microsoft and Anthropic accounting for 85% of $103 billion in total contract value with only $2.6 billion active at the end of August. Brookings put US AI infrastructure investment at $10.3 trillion from 2025 to 2032, an annual average of 3.63% of GDP, while writing that it would be premature to conclude that AI infrastructure already poses systemic risk comparable to earlier credit booms; Kansas City Fed President Jeff Schmid asked publicly whether the sector is moving to a too-big-to-fail ecosystem and announced no action. Epoch AI reported that the cost of a fixed level of AI performance has fallen about 47% per quarter since 2023. No financing has failed and no audited figure has been published for any of the revenue or burn projections."
   ],
   "changed": "The cost of the build-out became visible in the coupon: SoftBank paid its highest-ever dollar yields in the same week Treasury yields reached their highest since 2007. Anthropic's listing preparation moved to a founder-control share structure rather than a date, and a Federal Reserve bank president raised the too-big-to-fail question in public for the first time in this arc."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-10-05",
   "text": [
    "As of 5 October the most detailed account of a frontier lab's finances exists, and it is not a filing. Reuters reported in late September a prospectus it had seen showing revenue growing in 2025 to nearly $4.6 billion, and plans to spend $518 billion on cloud, computing and infrastructure obligations. CNBC reported that the near-$42 billion net loss included a large accounting charge reflecting an increase in the estimated value of convertible financing \"rather than money the company spent running its business\", and that the company lost more on an operating basis than that charge accounts for. CNBC reported that 80 of the prospectus's 261 pages are risk factors, warning of \"catastrophic or existential risk to humanity\", and TechCrunch reported Reuters' account of language on models that resist shutdown, conceal or manipulate information, and behave in ways resembling blackmail. The Financial Times said it had reviewed the filing and that nearly a third of it is risk factors. No registration statement appears on SEC EDGAR: a company search filtered to S-1 filings returns no matching companies, an unfiltered search returns only special-purpose vehicles and feeder funds, and a full-text search of S-1 filings across the week returns nothing.",
    "The debt behind the compute was reported for the first time. Quartz, citing Bloomberg, described a $60 billion package assembled around Anthropic's chip supply: a senior-secured tranche with banks preparing syndication and a junior tranche led by Blackstone putting up part of it from its own capital, covering roughly a third of what Anthropic has committed under a five-year lease for tensor processing unit capacity. The prospectus is reported to flag the supplier's dual position as chip vendor and lender as giving rise to potential conflicts of interest.",
    "Against that, the first serious public estimate of the revenue the build-out requires. Bain put the annual revenue the AI industry needs by 2031 to justify current data-centre investment at several times what existing consumer and enterprise services contribute, and described the remainder as a shortfall. Bloomberg reported OpenAI in early talks to raise around $30 billion at roughly a $1.4 trillion valuation. Nvidia's board added $150 billion to its buyback authorisation, taking the remaining total to $235 billion. No audited figure has been published for any lab's revenue or burn, and no financing has failed."
   ],
   "changed": "The arc's central question moved from whether Anthropic will list to whether the numbers already in circulation are the filed ones: EDGAR still shows no registration statement, and every figure rests on documents two newspapers say they reviewed. The week also exposed the lending structure under the compute, with a reported $60 billion package in which Anthropic's chip supplier is also its lender."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-10-12",
   "text": [
    "As of 12 October the arc's largest figures are all in newspapers and none in a filing. Reuters, citing the Financial Times, reported SpaceX planning to raise \"$40 billion for the purchase of Nvidia artificial intelligence chips\" — \"about $10 billion in bank loans and $30 billion in investment-grade debt\" — with Apollo Global Management expected to lead and PIMCO in talks on financing, and noted Morgan Stanley's estimate that \"AI infrastructure will require $1.5 trillion in external financing by 2028\". Benzinga relayed a Wall Street Journal report of Broadcom pursuing more than $50 billion to finance custom AI chips developed jointly with OpenAI, with Oracle negotiating separately with Apollo and Goldman Sachs. Lambda was reported raising up to $4 billion at a $14.5 billion pre-money valuation, with its backlog up from $15 billion in June to $50 billion in September and much of the increase traced to a $35 billion commitment from Anthropic. Bloomberg was reported putting DeepSeek close to at least 80 billion yuan and Moonshot AI at about $50 billion.",
    "The revenue that capital is measured against was cut, and the market moved on the day. TechCrunch reported the Financial Times saying OpenAI had told investors its annualised revenue is \"approaching $50 billion\", against a figure approaching $70 billion reported a little over a week earlier, and noted that Anthropic counts sales made by its cloud partners in its annualised revenue while OpenAI does not, so the two run rates are not directly comparable. SiliconANGLE put the Nasdaq's fall at \"1.25%\", \"its worst single-day performance since mid-August\", with CoreWeave down 8% and Oracle down 5.5% among the AI-linked decliners, and reported an anonymous source saying the lower figure was on a net-revenue basis while the higher one included gross revenue from OpenAI's partners.",
    "One listing did not happen, which is the first financing in this arc to fail outright. Data Center Dynamics reported that Firmus \"backed out of a planned IPO, blaming volatile market conditions\": it was due to list on October 23, the listing \"had been hoped\" to raise $5 billion, and the company had already cut its share price from AU$11 to AU$9 \"due to lack of interest from investors at home and abroad\", saying it \"will now pursue capital from the private markets and consider alternative public and private market options\". Firmus raised $2bn in August from backers including Nvidia and Blackstone at a post-money valuation of more than $10.5 billion. Reuters reported the Financial Times saying Nvidia was in early talks to deepen its investment in Reflection AI or acquire it, having already invested $800 million. No audited figure has been published for any lab's revenue or burn."
   ],
   "changed": "The capital column grew by two reported chip financings — $40 billion for SpaceX and more than $50 billion for Broadcom and OpenAI — while the revenue column was cut by $20 billion in a single report, and the Nasdaq fell the same day with CoreWeave down 8%. The arc also got its first outright failure: Firmus abandoned a $5 billion IPO after cutting its offer price twice."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-10-19",
   "text": [
    "As of 19 October the capital column and the market column moved in opposite directions in the same week. Anthropic told pre-IPO investors that third-quarter revenue was $16.8 billion, up from $11.6 billion in the second quarter and $4.7 billion in the first, that it was profitable on an adjusted basis, and that it generated $1.70 in revenue for every dollar spent on computing power. Investors discussed an IPO valuation near $1.5 trillion, down from the roughly $2 trillion previously expected. Anthropic is reported to be offering Blackstone a yield of more than 10% on $9 billion of chip lease payments. On the last day of the period Anthropic published a figure of its own: subject to stockholder approval, 5% of its shares go to a wholly owned public benefit fund, with an additional 2% at the board's discretion for every 50% increase in company value above an undisclosed baseline.",
    "Against that, CNBC reported that 20 of the most valuable global AI and semiconductor stocks shed more than $1 trillion in value in the week, with the Kospi closing down 5.46% on the Tuesday as SK hynix fell 10.09%. The yield to maturity on CoreWeave's 9.625% bond due in 2032 reached 14.8% as its shares fell 15.5%. Two forecasts arrived in the same days: Gartner's estimate that AI datacentre operators would need $8.5 trillion in revenue against at most $3.6 trillion available, with 80% of neoclouds not surviving as independent companies beyond 2030; and Jefferies' estimate that about half of the roughly $2.3 trillion contracted backlog at Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon and Oracle is owned by OpenAI and Anthropic. The IMF named an AI-related correction one of three salient risks to global financial stability.",
    "Two deals went the wrong way for the builders. Microsoft terminated its anchor-tenant agreement for 23,000 Nvidia chips at Nscale's Loughton site, and some Nscale backers are reported to want a $16bn to $19bn IPO valuation against the company's roughly $30bn goal. On the supply side ASML posted record third-quarter net sales of €11,662 million and said planned supply for 2027 and 2028 will not meet customer demand. No filing yet carries any of Anthropic's quarterly figures."

