  {
   "date": "2026-11-09",
   "text": [
    "As of 9 November one named tool appears in four independent records inside a single week, which is close to what the arc's question asked for. Check Point Research counted \"2,557 systems hosting or having hosted ARTEX panels\" and \"1,608 live and publicly available instances\", and describes one operator running two consoles in parallel through \"36 billion tokens\" for an estimated \"somewhere between $700 and $1500\", obtaining \"more than 300 certificates and their passwords\". Censys, the same day, observed \"1,465 hosts running an exposed ARTEX console\" of which roughly 815 look like distinct deployments, and says \"155 ARTEX hosts (10.6%) also run at least one other threat that Censys detects\". Microsoft said the actor it tracks as Storm-3358, \"a Cambodia-based intrusion cluster\", began targeting the financial sector in October 2026 using the framework, and separately observed an AI agent going from scanning GitHub for secrets to exfiltrating Azure Key Vault data \"in just five minutes\". India's CERT-In named an AI-assisted financial actor CRAFTY FALCON and warned of a \"significant escalation\".",
    "On the labs' side the published capability moved up a tier. OpenAI released GPT-6 Cyber at the Critical tier of its Preparedness Framework, reporting an intended-exploit success rate of 48.45% on ExploitGym against 42.43% for GPT-6 Astra, honeypot exploitation in 0.07% of graded rollouts without deployment safety instructions, 81 previously unknown high-severity Firefox flaws, and local privilege-escalation exploits for 948 distinct Linux kernel vulnerabilities at a cost of $1,213,430 in API credits — with access restricted through Daybreak Red and identity verification. Check Point reports the opposite selection pressure in the criminal market: guardrails were hit with Anthropic's Opus 5.5, after which the operator \"subsequently switched to more permissive Chinese models\". US prosecutors charged three men with using AI to impersonate chief executives and lawyers, taking about $129 million. Every count in the week is a vendor's, a victim's or a lab's own, and no government has published comparable attribution and counts of its own."
   ],
   "changed": "The question's first condition is close to met: two vendors published comparable censuses of the same framework on the same day — Check Point at 1,608 live instances, Censys at 1,465 exposed consoles — and Microsoft attached it to a named, located actor, Storm-3358, in the financial sector. A national CERT named an actor too. What is still missing is a government publishing its own counts. On the defenders' side the gap narrowed in a different direction: OpenAI's Critical-tier GPT-6 Cyber reports 948 distinct Linux kernel exploits for $1,213,430, against Check Point's estimate of $700 to $1500 for a criminal operator's two weeks of ARTEX work, and Check Point records that operator abandoning a Western model for Chinese ones after hitting guardrails."
  }
 ],
